As soon as we clear the top of the escarpment, we reach the slope of the Ngorongoro crater, the largest volcanic cauldron on earth and one of the most unique African ecosystems.
The forest that blankets the southern half of the crater is a direct result of the clouds that often get stuck on this side of the hill.
We are only three hours from the dry Serengeti plains but it feels like we are in the middle of the Amazon and as soon as we get to the edge of the crater it is going to be a totally different landscape.
